From 34ad98f902f1383b44fc5ae21ad917753a0727d1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 01:45:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] koblitz_fast: the scan's key changes basis by GFNI

FrobeniusCanon::canon_in_place took each key's normal coordinates by
eight byte-table lookups, about as many instructions as its rotation
loop.  The basis change is F_2-linear, so byte k of the coordinates is
a sum over the element's bytes of 8 x 8 blocks, which vgf2p8affineqb
applies to every byte of a lane: eight words are transposed as bytes
(vpermb), each byte lane is broadcast and multiplied by its column of
blocks, and the same transpose puts the words back.  Sixteen keys take
two transposes in, sixteen affine products and XORs, and two out.

The blocks come from the byte tables, which stay the basis's
description (the GPU fold and the external tables read them).  The
coordinates are the tables' word for word: tested at every degree from
2 to 63.  KIC_CANON_COORDS=tables keeps the tables as the same-binary
control.  Alone (gfnibench, isolated): 0.82-0.94 ns a key against
2.9-4.3 ns.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
